BRIEFS["10"] = ("Historical Assumption Archaeology", """Central hypothesis of the hunt and your job: the strongest unknown bug is probably NOT in the newest lines; it is in an old, trusted primitive whose assumptions were invalidated, exposed, recomposed or made economically exploitable by Upgrade 21. Inspect OLD code that U21 newly reaches or newly surrounds.

1. List files that did NOT change v8->v9 (ideally not since v7) but are called by, inherit into, or are called from files that DID change: `ResourceMetering.sol`, `universal/Proxy.sol`, `legacy/ResolvedDelegateProxy.sol`, `legacy/L1ChugSplashProxy.sol`, `legacy/AddressManager.sol`, `universal/StandardBridge.sol`, `OptimismMintableERC20Factory.sol`, `ReinitializableBase.sol`, `libraries/Hashing.sol`, `Encoding.sol`, `Types.sol`, `trie/MerkleTrie.sol`, `trie/SecureMerkleTrie.sol`, `rlp/RLPWriter.sol`, `Bytes.sol`, `AddressAliasHelper`, `EOA.sol`, `Storage.sol`, `dispute/DelayedWETH` base `WETH98`, `SuperchainConfig`.
2. For EVERY small library diff v8->v9 (`RLPReader`, `SafeCall`, `Burn`, `Constants`, `Predeploys`, `GasPayingToken`, `LibPosition`, `LibUDT`, `LibGameArgs`, `Types`, `MIPS64*`, `PreimageOracle`, `ProxyAdmin`, `ProxyAdminOwnedBase`, `LivenessModule`, `FeeVault`, `LegacyMintableERC20`): what changed (semantic vs formatting only), why, and which unchanged callers' assumptions it touches. In-flight dispute games: clones point at an implementation fixed at creation, but shared singletons (PreimageOracle, MIPS64, DelayedWETH proxy, ASR proxy) were upgraded under them - check at N-1 vs N which singletons unresolved pre-upgrade games reference and whether behaviour changed (DelayedWETH v2.0.0 has a new pause-identifier pointer while old games hold credit in it; ASR upgraded while old games call it in `closeGame`/`claimCredit`). Can old games still resolve and pay out? Can anyone now steal or permanently freeze bonds of in-flight games? Test `resolveClaim`/`resolve`/`closeGame`/`claimCredit` on real in-flight games with `vm.warp`.
3. Mine the v9 tree: `grep -rn "TODO\\|FIXME\\|XXX\\|HACK\\|nosemgrep\\|not strictly necessary\\|should never\\|cannot happen\\|assume\\|invariant\\|safe because\\|legacy\\|deprecated\\|spacer_" packages/contracts-bedrock/src`. For each hit in an L1 contract: did U21 falsify the stated reason (single portal, single registry, non-shared custody, non-interop, immutable -> mutable config, runs before/after a new external call, depends on a feature flag, interprets legacy storage)? `nosemgrep` justifications are prime targets.
4. Old code + new call order: `ResourceMetering.metered` + `depositTransaction` with the lockbox external call; `Proxy` admin-call semantics + `ProxyAdminOwnedBase` checks that compare proxy admins/owners across contracts - can a non-standard proxy type (ResolvedDelegateProxy for L1CDM, L1ChugSplashProxy for L1StandardBridge) answer those queries differently? Any "A.proxyAdminOwner() == B.proxyAdminOwner()" check where one side is attacker-deployable is an authorization-bypass candidate: find every such sameness check in v9 and what it gates.
5. Add to each PRIM file: OLD ASSUMPTION / NEW U21 CONDITION / COMPOSITION POSSIBILITY / REQUIRED ATTACKER CONTROL / POSSIBLE IMPACT.""")

BRIEFS["11"] = ("Invariant and Differential Fuzzing", """Executable invariant and differential suites against the REAL deployed post-U21 implementations, with pre-upgrade behaviour (block N-1 fork) as the differential baseline. Save useful suites under your mirror directory.

1. Build a real-withdrawal fixture first: from mainnet logs (`https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com`, `cast logs`, <=10k-block windows over roughly the last 60k blocks) find OP Mainnet portal withdrawals that are (a) proven before U21, mature or nearly mature, not finalized; (b) proven after U21 against a type-9 game, not finalized; (c) messenger withdrawals (sender `0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000007`, target L1CDM) and plain ones, with and without value. Events: `WithdrawalProven(bytes32 indexed, address indexed from, address indexed to)`, `WithdrawalProvenExtension1(bytes32 indexed, address indexed proofSubmitter)`, `WithdrawalFinalized(bytes32 indexed, bool)`. Reconstruct each `WithdrawalTransaction` by decoding the L1 prove tx calldata. Store fixtures as JSON; write a Solidity base test that loads one, warps past maturity, resolves the real game through public functions if needed (document any prank precisely) and finalizes on the fork. Prove it end-to-end at least once.
2. Invariants around real finalizations and deposits (handlers: deposit with/without value, donateETH, prove replays by other submitters, finalize, finalizeExternalProof, deleteProvenWithdrawal, warp, adversarial targets via `vm.etch`: reverting, gas-burning, return-bomb, re-entering every portal/lockbox/messenger entry point): ETH conservation (delta lockbox + delta portal == deposits - delivered value; portal balance restored after every call); withdrawal uniqueness across finalize / external proof / messenger replay; proof isolation between submitters; finalized => delivered-or-replayable for messenger withdrawals; pause consistency (prank guardian to pause the lockbox identifier, then every value-moving entry point must revert); portal/L1CDM replay consistency.
3. DIFFERENTIAL: same fixture + same action at N-1 (v8 code) and at N / 26433900 (v9 code); diff outcomes (success/revert selector, events, balance deltas, gas). Every difference must be explained by a documented U21 change; an unexplained difference is a primitive. Include: finalize with a failing messenger call; binary-search minimum gas for success on both; value withdrawals to empty accounts (v9 adds 183,600 to the `callWithMinGas` requirement when target.codehash == 0); large-calldata deposits; re-prove behaviour; view getters.
4. Gas-boundary fuzz on the real messenger path: fuzz gas supplied to `finalizeWithdrawalTransaction` and classify {reverted, finalized+relayed, finalized+failedMessages, finalized with NEITHER recorded}. The last class existing at ANY gas value under v9 is a serious primitive - report exact gas values. Be honest about coverage limits and run counts.""")

BRIEFS["12"] = ("Negative-Space and Omitted-Check Analysis", """Every security check ADDED, REMOVED, MOVED or WEAKENED between v8 and v9 is a signpost. Enumerate them all and analyse the negative space around each.

1. Produce the complete v8->v9 diff of `packages/contracts-bedrock/src/{L1,dispute,universal,libraries,legacy,safe}` and of `interfaces/` (added/removed `error`, `event`, `function` - new custom errors index new checks). Build a table in your lane report: one row per added/removed/changed require / revert / modifier / access-control check, with file:line in v9 (and v8 for removed ones).
2. For EVERY row answer concretely: (1) why it was added; (2) which SIBLING path lacks it (prove vs super-root prove; finalize vs finalizeExternalProof; lockETH vs receiveLiquidity vs donateETH; authorizePortal vs authorizeLockbox; initialize vs upgrade; FaultDisputeGame vs Super vs Permissioned vs Super-Permissioned vs ZK; L1 vs L2 messenger/bridge; OPCM upgrade vs migrate vs deploy); (3) does it run before or after the mutation / external call it protects; (4) is there an equivalent operation through ANOTHER contract that skips it; (5) does it read mutable CURRENT configuration to judge HISTORICAL state; (6) is the validated object the exact object later used; (7) can an upgrade/migration bypass it. For REMOVED checks: what did it protect and what replaces it - does the replacement cover every case (bad-target list, reentrancy guard, dropped `whenNotPaused`, `l2Sender` guard, nonzero / code-length checks, same-owner checks, chain-id checks, registry checks, external-call success handling)?
3. Special attention: nonzero, code-length, shared-owner, authorization, feature, pause, chain-ID, registry checks and external-call success handling (ignored low-level return values, mishandled `SafeCall` results, swallowing try/catch).
4. For the ~10 most suspicious rows write a fork test against the deployed implementations that attempts the sibling/bypass path and records the real outcome. A check present in source but ineffective on chain, or a sibling path that succeeds where the checked path reverts, is a primitive. Killed ideas become REJ files with the exact killing fact.
5. Validator-only checks: assertions that exist only in `OPContractsManagerStandardValidator` / `MigrationValidator` and are NOT enforced by the contracts - can a permissionless actor make on-chain state violate one after validation passed? Evaluate a handful against mainnet state for all four chains.""")

BRIEFS["13"] = ("Scope Snapshot", """The blind-first restriction on prior audits does not apply to SCOPE material (programme page, governance proposal), but do not dig into audit findings. Write `SCOPE_SNAPSHOT.md` (your mirror copy), dated 2026-11-17, with a source URL and retrieval status for every item:

1. Current Immunefi Optimism bug-bounty scope: fetch `https://immunefi.com/bug-bounty/optimism/` and its scope/information sub-pages (follow redirects; if the HTML is a JS shell, extract embedded JSON such as `__NEXT_DATA__`). Record exact in-scope assets (addresses, names, types), in-scope impacts per severity, reward ranges, PoC requirements, Primacy of Impact vs Primacy of Rules, the known-issue / out-of-scope list verbatim, prohibited activities, and any wording about upgraded implementations behind listed proxies. If a page cannot be retrieved, state exactly what failed and fall back to other official sources (docs.optimism.io security pages, the monorepo `SECURITY.md` and security policy docs under `/home/user/ethereum-optimism/optimism`), labelling anything not read from the live programme page UNVERIFIED-AGAINST-LIVE-PAGE. Never invent scope text.
2. The official Protocol Upgrade Proposal for Upgrade 21 on `https://gov.optimism.io` (Discourse JSON: `/search.json?q=...`, `/t/<slug>/<id>.json`) and the on-chain vote on `https://vote.optimism.io`: URL, dates, the exact tag/commit covered (`op-contracts/v9.0.0`), listed contract versions and implementation addresses, superchain-ops task reference, audit references (titles only), chains. Cross-check with `docs/public-docs/notices/upgrade-21.mdx`, with `snapshots/semver-lock.json` in the v9 checkout, and with what LANE_CONTEXT.md records from chain.
3. Asset-eligibility table for the contracts in LANE_CONTEXT.md section 3 (OP Mainnet proxies and new implementations; sibling chains Ink/Soneium/Unichain): (a) explicitly listed, (b) official upgrade code behind a listed proxy, (c) Primacy of Impact only, (d) out of scope / unknown - with the evidence line. Be conservative.
4. A "SCOPE PROOF" template (asset, proposal, deployed proxy, implementation, affected chain, impact class) pre-filled for OptimismPortal, SystemConfig, ETHLockbox, L1CrossDomainMessenger, AnchorStateRegistry, DisputeGameFactory.
Final answer <= 300 words: what was retrieved live vs not, headline scope facts, and scope risks for this hunt.""")


def main() -> None:
    out = Path(sys.argv[1])
    out.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    for orig, (title, brief) in BRIEFS.items():
        num = str(int(orig) + 20)
        text = PREAMBLE.format(
            lane_name=f"CODEX MIRROR LANE {num} - \"{title}\" (mirror of lane {orig})",
            hunt=HUNT,
            orig=orig,
            num=num,
            port_lo=f"18{num}0",
            port_hi=f"18{num}9",
            brief=brief,
        )
        (out / f"CODEX-LANE-{num}.prompt.md").write_text(text)
        print(f"lane {num} <- {orig}: {title} ({len(text)} chars)")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
